## Solution

**Claim.** The one-point compactification of $S^n \times \mathbb{R}$ is homeomorphic to $S^{n+1}$ with two distinct points identified to a single point, i.e. the quotient $S^{n+1}/\{p\sim q\}$ for any two distinct $p,q\in S^{n+1}$.

**Proof.** The proof proceeds in three steps.

**Step 1. $S^n\times\mathbb{R}\cong\mathbb{R}^{n+1}\setminus\{0\}$.**

View $S^n\subset\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ as the unit sphere. Define
$$\Phi:S^n\times\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}^{n+1}\setminus\{0\},\qquad \Phi(u,t)=e^t\,u.$$
This is continuous, with continuous inverse
$$\Phi^{-1}(x)=\Bigl(\frac{x}{\lVert x\rVert},\,\ln\lVert x\rVert\Bigr),\qquad x\in\mathbb{R}^{n+1}\setminus\{0\}.$$
Hence $\Phi$ is a homeomorphism.

**Step 2. $\mathbb{R}^{n+1}\setminus\{0\}\cong S^{n+1}\setminus\{N,S\}$.**

Let $S^{n+1}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n+2}$ with north pole $N=(0,\dots,0,1)$ and south pole $S=(0,\dots,0,-1)$. Stereographic projection from $N$,
$$\sigma:S^{n+1}\setminus\{N\}\xrightarrow{\;\cong\;}\mathbb{R}^{n+1},$$
is a homeomorphism sending $S$ to the origin $0\in\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$. Restricting to $S^{n+1}\setminus\{N,S\}$ gives a homeomorphism
$$S^{n+1}\setminus\{N,S\}\cong\mathbb{R}^{n+1}\setminus\{0\}.$$

Combining Steps 1 and 2, $S^n\times\mathbb{R}\cong S^{n+1}\setminus\{N,S\}$.

**Step 3. The one-point compactification of $S^{n+1}\setminus\{N,S\}$ is $S^{n+1}/\{N\sim S\}$.**

The space $X:=S^{n+1}\setminus\{N,S\}$ is locally compact Hausdorff (open subset of compact Hausdorff), so its one-point compactification $X^*=X\cup\{\infty\}$ is compact Hausdorff.

Define $\pi:S^{n+1}\to X^*$ by
$$\pi(x)=\begin{cases}x,&x\in X=S^{n+1}\setminus\{N,S\},\\ \infty,&x\in\{N,S\}.\end{cases}$$

*Continuity.* On the open set $X\subset X^*$, $\pi$ is the identity (into $X$), so $\pi^{-1}(U)$ is open in $S^{n+1}$ for every open $U\subset X$. A basic neighborhood of $\infty$ in $X^*$ has the form $\{\infty\}\cup(X\setminus K)$ for $K\subset X$ compact. Its preimage is
$$\pi^{-1}\bigl(\{\infty\}\cup(X\setminus K)\bigr)=\{N,S\}\cup(X\setminus K)=S^{n+1}\setminus K.$$
Since $K$ is compact in $X\subset S^{n+1}$, it is compact (hence closed) in $S^{n+1}$, so $S^{n+1}\setminus K$ is open. Thus $\pi$ is continuous.

*Quotient property.* $\pi$ is a continuous surjection from the compact space $S^{n+1}$ onto the Hausdorff space $X^*$, hence it is a closed (and therefore quotient) map. Consequently
$$X^*\cong S^{n+1}\big/\{N\sim S\}.$$

**Conclusion.** Since $S^{n+1}$ is homogeneous under its isometry group, the quotient $S^{n+1}/\{p\sim q\}$ is, up to homeomorphism, independent of the choice of distinct points $p,q$. Therefore:

$$\boxed{\bigl(S^n\times\mathbb{R}\bigr)^*\;\cong\; S^{n+1}\big/\{p\sim q\},\quad p\neq q\in S^{n+1},}$$

i.e. the one-point compactification of $S^n\times\mathbb{R}$ is the $(n+1)$-sphere with two distinct points identified to one point (a "pinched" sphere).

*Sanity check ($n=0$).* $S^0\times\mathbb{R}$ is two disjoint lines; its one-point compactification is two circles sharing a single common point, i.e. $S^1\vee S^1$. On the other hand $S^1$ with two points identified is also a figure-eight $S^1\vee S^1$. The two agree. $\checkmark$

